# {{RUNTIME_NAME}}

The Sidekicks Framework, packaged as a **CLI working folder**: a read-only framework core you mount
into your own repository as a git submodule, plus a one-line installer.

Generated by `sk-inherit` at {{GENERATED_AT}} from source commit `{{SOURCE_COMMIT}}`.
Framework version **{{CORE_VERSION}}** · {{SKILL_COUNT}} skills.

> Everything in this repository is generated. Fixes belong in the Sidekicks source repository and
> arrive here as a new release — see [Contributing](#contributing).

## Install — start here

**This one line is the install.** It is the supported path on every platform; everything else in
this README describes a workspace the installer has already created.

```sh
curl -fsSL {{RAW_INSTALL_URL}} | sh -s -- --ref {{CORE_TAG}}
```

> **The `--ref` is not optional decoration.** This README describes **{{CORE_TAG}}**. Without it the
> installer mounts whatever `{{DEFAULT_REF}}` currently serves, which is a different version
> whenever a release has been tagged but not pushed — and every command, path and count documented
> below would then describe something you did not install. Verify what you got with
> `node bin/sidekicks core status`.
>
> The two refs in that line are deliberately different. The *script* is fetched from
> `{{DEFAULT_REF}}` because it is generic — it mounts whatever `--ref` names — and a first-time
> reader has no tag to fetch it from. The *framework* it mounts is `{{CORE_TAG}}`. `release` pushes
> the tag before the branch, so a README that reaches `{{DEFAULT_REF}}` can only ever name a tag the
> remote already serves.

> **Cloning this repository is not an install.** A clone *is* the framework core — the very thing
> the installer mounts at `{{CORE_DIR}}/` — so it has no `projects/`, no workspace `.sidekicks/`,
> and no `{{DEFAULT_DIR}}/` directory inside it. Run the installer instead; it creates
> `{{DEFAULT_DIR}}/` (rename with `--dir`) and mounts this repository at `{{CORE_DIR}}/`, which is
> dot-prefixed and therefore invisible to a plain `ls`.

With options (the installer reads them from `sh -s --`):

```sh
curl -fsSL {{RAW_INSTALL_URL}} | sh -s -- --dir my-workspace --ref {{CORE_TAG}}
```

Windows (PowerShell):

```powershell
irm {{RAW_INSTALL_PS1_URL}} -OutFile install.ps1
./install.ps1 -Dir my-workspace -Ref {{CORE_TAG}}
```

| Option | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--dir <path>` | workspace directory (default `{{DEFAULT_DIR}}`) |
| `--ref <ref>` | framework tag or branch to pin (default `{{DEFAULT_REF}}`; this release is `{{CORE_TAG}}`) |
| `--remote <url>` | framework remote to mount (default this repository) |
| `--no-init` | mount only; run `core init` yourself |

**The installer commits nothing.** It stages the submodule and leaves the seeded workspace files
untracked, so the first commit in your new repo is yours to review and make:

```sh
cd {{DEFAULT_DIR}}
git status                 # the mount, staged; the seeded files, untracked
git add -A
git commit -m "chore: bootstrap sidekicks workspace"
```

Requirements: **git** and **Node.js ≥ 20**. Nothing else — the framework has zero npm runtime
dependencies, so there is no install step, no build and no bundler.

**Windows: turn on Developer Mode if you can.** The per-CLI skill directories are junctions, which
need no privilege, but `CLAUDE.md` and `GEMINI.md` are *file* links to `AGENTS.md`, and Windows only
lets an unprivileged process create one with Developer Mode on (Settings → Privacy & security → For
developers). Without it those two are written as copies instead: everything works, and `core init` /
`core update` rewrite them whenever `AGENTS.md` has moved on, but between those runs a CLI reading
its own copy can be looking at an older instruction file than the one you just edited. `core doctor`
reports the divergence when it happens.

